<%# The booking popup: Google's appointment page in a dialog, opened by any
    a[data-booking-popup] link on the page (studio/booking/_assets carries the
    script). Rendered once per page by `studio_booking_popup`; the site footer
    calls it.

    It does what Google's own scheduling-button script does, without loading
    that script, its stylesheet and its icon font on every public page: the
    frame is requested only when the dialog first opens. The links keep their
    own href, so with scripts off, or on a modified click, they are ordinary
    links to the booking page.

    A NATIVE <dialog>, NOT THE MODAL HOST (studio/modals/_host), on purpose. The
    host needs Alpine, its store, and each modal registered inside the host's
    block in the layout, so the footer would stop being one layout line and an
    app with no host would have no popup. Its card is padded and scrolls, where
    this body is a full-bleed frame. And it mounts content through
    <template x-if>, which would build a new frame, and ask Google again, on
    every open; this dialog keeps the loaded one. showModal() gives the top
    layer, the inert page and the focus trap without any of that.

    ESCAPE HAS A LIMIT NOTHING HERE CAN LIFT. Once focus is inside Google's
    frame, key presses go to Google (it is another origin), so Escape no longer
    reaches the dialog. The Close button and a click on the backdrop always
    work, which is why the button is a solid one. %>
<%= studio_booking_assets %>
<dialog class="booking-popup" data-booking-dialog aria-label="<%= label %>">
  <form method="dialog" class="booking-popup-bar">
    <span class="booking-popup-title"><%= label %></span>
    <button type="submit" class="booking-popup-close" data-booking-close>Close ✕</button>
  </form>
  <iframe data-booking-popup-frame
          data-src="<%= studio_booking_embed_url %>"
          title="<%= title %>" frameborder="0"></iframe>
</dialog>
